  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nWhen curl follows an HTTPS redirect through an HTTP CONNECT proxy, a stale response code lets the proxy's CONNECT response `Content-Disposition` header control the local output filename. With `--location --remote-name --remote-header-name`, the proxy-provided filename is used for the TLS-verified origin's response body, crossing the CONNECT/origin trust boundary.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`Curl_req_soft_reset()` in `lib/request.c` does not clear `data->info.httpcode`. After the first origin's 302 response, this stale value persists while curl establishes a new CONNECT tunnel for the redirect target.\n\n`single_header()` in `lib/cf-h1-proxy.c` delivers CONNECT response headers to the header callback with `CLIENTWRITE_CONNECT`, but this provenance bit is internal to the writer chain — it never reaches `CURLOPT_HEADERFUNCTION`. The stale httpcode is only cleared when the tunnel reaches `H1_TUNNEL_ESTABLISHED`, after headers have already been delivered.\n\n`tool_header_cb()` in `src/tool_cb_hdr.c` calls `CURLINFO_RESPONSE_CODE`, sees the stale 302, and passes the proxy's `Content-Disposition` header to `content_disposition()`, which opens the output file under the proxy-chosen name and disables further filename handling. The real origin's `Content-Disposition` header arrives later but is ignored.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\nThe PoC uses two certificate-verified HTTPS origins and an HTTP/1.1 CONNECT proxy, all on loopback. Origin 1 returns a 302 redirect to Origin 2. The proxy injects `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=\"proxy-chosen.bin\"` in the CONNECT response for Origin 2. Origin 2 returns `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=\"origin-name.bin\"` with a body.\n\n\n### Expected vs actual output\n\n**Expected:** All three cases produce `origin-name.bin` — the filename from the TLS-verified origin.\n\n**Actual:** The redirect case produces `proxy-chosen.bin` containing the authenticated origin's body. The two controls correctly produce `origin-name.bin`. This shows the proxy's CONNECT response header overwrites the filename only when a stale 3xx code is present from a prior redirect.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nPrevent CONNECT-provenance headers from participating in `--remote-header-name` processing. The strongest fix would propagate header provenance to the command-line callback so it can distinguish CONNECT headers from origin headers. Alternatively, clearing `data->info.httpcode` before CONNECT headers are delivered (rather than after, in `H1_TUNNEL_ESTABLISHED`) would close this specific trigger.\n\n## Impact\n\nA malicious HTTP proxy (or an on-path attacker against the plaintext proxy hop) can choose the local basename for data returned by an authenticated HTTPS origin. The attacker cannot modify the TLS-protected response body, but controls the name under which curl stores it.\n\nThe filename is subject to curl's basename sanitization. Existing files are preserved by default; `--clobber` permits replacement. Using `--suppress-connect-headers` mitigates the issue.",
